1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,180
Well, good morning, church.

2
00:00:17,180 --> 00:00:21,640
Thanks so much for joining us this morning.

3
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:25,800
Excited to continue our series that we have been going through,

4
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,300
the spiritual fruit in Galatians chapter 5.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,580
And, man, Donovan, I believe that you heard correctly from the Lord

6
00:00:34,580 --> 00:00:39,660
because that song, the chorus of that song literally is the outline of my message.

7
00:00:39,660 --> 00:00:42,580
And so maybe I should just not preach and we should just keep singing.

8
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:47,420
But since I'm contractually obligated, I will.

9
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:51,020
I'll give the message and then we might have to run that one back again at the end.

10
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:54,420
But we've been in this series through the spiritual fruit.

11
00:00:54,420 --> 00:00:59,820
And it's important that we keep reminding ourselves of that beginning message.

12
00:00:59,820 --> 00:01:04,340
At the beginning of this series, we said that it's really the central idea

13
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:09,840
behind this entire passage in Galatians chapter 5 is that illustration of fruits.

14
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:15,280
That Paul is trying to make it very clear to believers that you can't do this thing on your own.

15
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:18,660
You can't be patient and kind and gentle.

16
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:23,340
You can't have all of these characteristics manifested in your life on your own.

17
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:26,860
Rather, it is you abiding in Jesus.

18
00:01:26,860 --> 00:01:30,740
And he is the source, the giver of all of these things.

19
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:33,780
He is the perfect manifestation of all of these things.

20
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:41,280
And it's when we root ourselves in who Jesus is that these fruits are produced in our life.

21
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:46,000
And so each time as we look at each individual fruit, it's just important that we remember

22
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,060
that this isn't something that we kind of white-knuckle ourselves into being a more loving person

23
00:01:51,060 --> 00:01:55,600
or a more patient person, but that we regularly come back to the foot of the cross.

24
00:01:56,640 --> 00:01:58,140
Remember who Jesus is.

25
00:01:58,140 --> 00:02:00,060
Remember what he's done for us.

26
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:03,440
And allow him to produce that from the inside out.

27
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:09,360
So let me pray, and then we'll hop into this week's characteristic, which is goodness.

28
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:14,580
Father, we thank you for this opportunity that we have to gather together this morning

29
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:24,680
to remember your goodness, to try and wrap our human brains around a concept that is far too great for us to understand.

30
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:34,920
God, may you give us wisdom to understand the fullness of your goodness, what it means for us in a broken world.

31
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:42,140
May we wrestle with your goodness even as we struggle to see it in the brokenness of a sinful world.

32
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:45,100
Holy Spirit, we need to hear from you this morning.

33
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:46,460
We do not need to hear from me.

34
00:02:46,460 --> 00:02:55,140
And so, God, I often pray that in the same way that people heard Peter's first sermon in their own dialects and languages,

35
00:02:55,140 --> 00:02:59,840
that this morning your Holy Spirit would speak individually to each one of us what we need to hear.

36
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:03,860
God, make that happen for your glory and for our good.

37
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:05,080
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

38
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:05,980
Amen.

39
00:03:07,880 --> 00:03:16,080
Well, have any of you ever started a project that you thought was going to be simple, easy, not take very much time,

40
00:03:16,080 --> 00:03:22,920
and then gotten into it and realized that it was far more difficult and time-consuming than you thought it was going to be?

41
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:25,300
Anybody ever had that experience?

42
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:31,140
Last, a couple weeks ago, my wife has been wanting these closet shelves in our kitchen closet.

43
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:39,560
And I've told you many times before, I am not handy, I am not good with tools, but I thought closet shelves can't be that difficult.

44
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:43,920
I thought it was going to be, she was going out of town, I was like, I'm going to surprise her, I'm going to get these closet shelves put in,

45
00:03:43,920 --> 00:03:45,220
she's going to come home, it's going to be great.

46
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:54,980
But because I'm a realistic person, I also invited my dad with his tools because, you know, I figured, but it was closet shelves, right?

47
00:03:54,980 --> 00:03:56,120
Like, how difficult could it be?

48
00:03:56,120 --> 00:04:00,880
They come with one package of hardware and, like, two suggested tools that you need.

49
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:06,120
So I was thinking, these two tools, this one package of hardware, and an hour, and we're done.

50
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:18,760
Well, like, four different hodgepodge packets of hardware that we combined together, like, eight tools, six of which were not included in the necessary tool requirements.

51
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:26,440
And about four hours later, I got some semblance of closet shelves put in my kitchen closet.

52
00:04:26,440 --> 00:04:34,180
It was something that I thought at the beginning was going to be really simple, but actually ended up being far more complex than I ever realized.

53
00:04:34,180 --> 00:04:40,020
So maybe you started a hobby that you thought was just going to be a nice addition to your life, right?

54
00:04:40,020 --> 00:04:42,460
Just a little time-consuming thing that you would enjoy.

55
00:04:42,460 --> 00:04:48,440
And then upon starting the hobby, it kind of opened you up to a world of new information.

56
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:52,180
And, like, you could lose yourself in this hobby for the rest of your life.

57
00:04:52,180 --> 00:04:54,320
Anybody had this experience with a hobby?

58
00:04:54,320 --> 00:05:03,720
Marissa and I were at the Cape and Resort in Berkeley Springs a few months ago, and there were these quilters in this, like, they rented out the room, right?

59
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:12,160
And, like, what I learned this weekend as interacting with these quilters, and, like, they were more than happy to tell me about this world of quilting, right?

60
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:15,560
It's just, like, it's just, it's just village.

61
00:05:15,560 --> 00:05:19,680
It's just, like, it's just so much information about quilting.

62
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:23,100
And my favorite stories, I saw this lady, they were there day in and day out.

63
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:25,040
We would wake up at 7 a.m., they were in there quilting.

64
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:27,480
We would come back at 9 p.m., they were in there quilting.

65
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:36,160
And one time I saw a lady taking a break from quilting, and she was in the little library with a magazine, and the magazine was about quilting.

66
00:05:37,240 --> 00:05:51,180
She was taking a break from quilting to read about quilting, and the reason I share these stories with you at the outset of this message on goodness is because that's kind of how I felt about this idea of goodness this week as I studied it.

67
00:05:51,880 --> 00:05:58,060
So I'm being honest, goodness just kind of feels like a Christian cliche word, like, with not a whole lot of substance.

68
00:05:58,060 --> 00:06:10,180
I thought I would get into the study, and it would be a nice thing for us to talk about for a week, but was it really going to have, like, long-lasting implications on our life to talk about this idea of goodness?

69
00:06:10,180 --> 00:06:22,760
I mean, even as I read it in the context in the passage in Galatians chapter 5, the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, and self-control.

70
00:06:22,760 --> 00:06:34,060
Even where it finds itself in the list, it kind of just feels like a poetic filler, like a nice word that just kind of adds to the concept but doesn't have that much substance.

71
00:06:34,060 --> 00:06:37,780
We even kind of trivialize the word good in our own culture.

72
00:06:37,780 --> 00:06:40,540
We read books, like, from good to great.

73
00:06:40,540 --> 00:06:45,720
We talk about good food, good music, good bands, good grades.

74
00:06:45,720 --> 00:06:51,940
And for whatever reason, goodness has become, like, a B-minus quality word for us, right?

75
00:06:51,940 --> 00:06:55,840
Like, it's just, like, the bottom of the goodness spectrum.

76
00:06:56,460 --> 00:07:14,160
However, as I studied this passage, as I studied this word goodness in its biblical context, I was blown away by all of the ideas and implications that are built into this one simple word.

77
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:19,960
I think it's honestly the most complicated characteristic of the fruits of the Spirit for us to pull off.

78
00:07:19,960 --> 00:07:33,780
We're going to get into that a little bit more, but it's so all-encompassing that for us to kind of wrap our minds around it and feel good about, like, manifesting goodness in our life, I think it's really difficult.

79
00:07:33,780 --> 00:07:49,660
And what I believe is that our hope and pray is that as we get into this study, it's going to open up our lives to a lifelong pursuit of understanding what it looks like to manifest the goodness of God in our everyday lives.

80
00:07:49,660 --> 00:07:51,800
It's not some simple cliche word.

81
00:07:51,800 --> 00:07:55,300
It is far-reaching and radical.

82
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:57,900
So, let's get into it.

83
00:07:57,900 --> 00:08:01,980
Let's talk about what it means and how it might impact our lives.

84
00:08:01,980 --> 00:08:06,880
God's goodness literally starts at the beginning of the pages of Scripture.

85
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:11,220
The first two chapters in Genesis are filled with this word good.

86
00:08:11,220 --> 00:08:14,900
God declaring that his creation is good.

87
00:08:14,900 --> 00:08:20,620
Genesis 1 and 2, we read the word good seven times in the first two chapters of Scripture.

88
00:08:20,620 --> 00:08:22,400
It says, and God created the light.

89
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:23,840
He saw that light was good.

90
00:08:24,180 --> 00:08:27,040
God saw that dry land and water was good.

91
00:08:27,040 --> 00:08:30,040
God saw that plants and trees, they were good.

92
00:08:30,040 --> 00:08:34,680
He saw the birds of the air and the fish of the sea, and they were good.

93
00:08:34,680 --> 00:08:41,580
He created us, male and female, in his image, giving us dominion over all of creation.

94
00:08:41,580 --> 00:08:45,660
And he declared at the end of it all that it was very good.

95
00:08:45,660 --> 00:08:52,900
From the beginning, God has been demonstrating his goodness to us through his creation.

96
00:08:53,900 --> 00:08:56,480
He created human beings in his image.

97
00:08:56,480 --> 00:08:59,900
And he gave us creativity to steward creation.

98
00:08:59,900 --> 00:09:04,460
He put us in a perfect garden in harmony with one another and with creation.

99
00:09:04,460 --> 00:09:07,920
And he said that it was good.

100
00:09:09,100 --> 00:09:15,220
All of this was a manifestation of God's goodness, but there's only one problem, right?

101
00:09:15,220 --> 00:09:21,780
You guys have read, like, those of you that have tried to read Scripture through the Bible in a year, I know you've made it at least this far.

102
00:09:21,780 --> 00:09:28,280
Like, the first three pages of Scripture, we've got goodness all over the first two chapters of creation.

103
00:09:28,280 --> 00:09:33,400
And then in an instant, goodness is shattered.

104
00:09:33,400 --> 00:09:38,740
God's creation decides that they are going to redefine goodness in their own image.

105
00:09:39,740 --> 00:09:46,020
At the root of the serpent's temptation in the garden is this doubt that God is good.

106
00:09:46,020 --> 00:09:54,300
He's putting the seed of doubt in the mind of Adam and Eve that God might not be good, that he might try to be withholding them from something.

107
00:09:55,960 --> 00:10:01,300
And Adam and Eve eventually believe this lie, that God is trying to withhold good things from them.

108
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:08,140
And they decide that they are going to redefine goodness in their own image.

109
00:10:08,140 --> 00:10:11,820
See, biblical goodness is rooted in God.

110
00:10:11,820 --> 00:10:22,800
But we've spent the rest of creation from Genesis chapter 3 until now redefining goodness in our own image to fit our own narrative, our own agenda,

111
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:31,100
to make us feel like we are justified to do the things that we want to do, do the things we want to do the way that we want to do them.

112
00:10:31,100 --> 00:10:35,820
We redefine goodness in our own image.

113
00:10:35,820 --> 00:10:38,540
It starts with Adam and Eve, but we can't blame them.

114
00:10:38,540 --> 00:10:41,800
We've got to take credit where credit is due.

115
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:51,440
And there's this awesome passage in Romans chapter 1 that pretty much articulates that ever since Adam and Eve decided to redefine goodness in their own image,

116
00:10:51,640 --> 00:10:54,860
we've taken up that mantle and risen with it, right?

117
00:10:54,860 --> 00:11:03,300
Like we, culture by culture, generation by generation, continue this pattern of redefining God's goodness in our own image.

118
00:11:03,300 --> 00:11:06,300
Romans chapter 1, verse 18 says this,

119
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:12,180
But God chose his anger from heaven against all sinful and wicked people who do what?

120
00:11:12,180 --> 00:11:13,860
They suppress the truth.

121
00:11:13,940 --> 00:11:17,720
The truth is that God is good, that his definition of goodness matters.

122
00:11:17,720 --> 00:11:24,160
But by their wickedness, they know the truth about God because he's made it obvious to them in creation.

123
00:11:24,160 --> 00:11:30,760
And through his interacting with the nation of Israel and sending Jesus, he's made his goodness clear to us.

124
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:43,780
But ever since the world was created, people have seen the earth and sky, we've seen everything that God has made, we can clearly see God's invisible qualities, his eternal power, and his divine nature.

125
00:11:43,780 --> 00:11:48,040
So we have no excuse for not knowing God.

126
00:11:48,040 --> 00:11:54,120
Yes, we knew God, but we wouldn't worship him as God or even give him thanks.

127
00:11:54,220 --> 00:11:57,580
We begin to think up foolish ideas of what God was like.

128
00:11:57,580 --> 00:12:00,720
See this redefinition of goodness in Romans chapter 1?

129
00:12:00,720 --> 00:12:07,100
It's humanity thinking up foolish ideas of what God is like, redefining God's goodness in our own image.

130
00:12:07,100 --> 00:12:08,000
It says,

131
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,860
As a result, their minds became dark and confused, claiming to be wise.

132
00:12:11,860 --> 00:12:22,380
They instead became utter fools, and instead of worshiping the glorious, ever-living God, they worshiped idols made to look like mere people and birds and animals and reptiles.

133
00:12:22,540 --> 00:12:29,460
We have taken this mantle of redefining God's goodness in our own image, and we continue to do it.

134
00:12:29,460 --> 00:12:34,360
We think about foolish ideas about what God is like.

135
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:45,460
We create religions that we can control so that we get to define what is right and wrong and take that responsibility out of God's hands.

136
00:12:45,460 --> 00:12:49,220
So far, this is a lot of bad news, right?

137
00:12:49,780 --> 00:12:52,780
This is a lot of negative talk.

138
00:12:52,780 --> 00:12:56,920
But God was not caught off guard by our redefinition of goodness.

139
00:12:56,920 --> 00:12:59,340
It wasn't a surprise to him.

140
00:12:59,340 --> 00:13:04,240
And from the moment we rebelled, God began his plan of recreating.

141
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:10,880
Recreating things back into his image, back into his goodness.

142
00:13:11,040 --> 00:13:14,040
We read at the very end of Scripture.

143
00:13:14,040 --> 00:13:18,280
For those of you that made it that far in your Scripture reading, I'll give you a quick hand, right?

144
00:13:18,280 --> 00:13:19,200
Like, it's not easy.

145
00:13:19,200 --> 00:13:21,600
We got through the first three chapters of Genesis.

146
00:13:21,600 --> 00:13:23,120
Now we're at the end of Revelation.

147
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:31,880
At the end of it all, we see God recreating a new heaven and a new earth, restoring creation to the goodness that he intended.

148
00:13:33,380 --> 00:13:39,240
And he allows us to participate in this process of recreation.

149
00:13:39,240 --> 00:13:44,740
The new heaven and the new earth is the only thing that could be better than the Garden of Eden.

150
00:13:46,560 --> 00:13:49,660
So God has this ultimate plan to restore his goodness.

151
00:13:49,660 --> 00:13:52,620
And the new heaven and the new earth is going to be better than the Garden of Eden.

152
00:13:52,620 --> 00:13:56,320
Because in the Garden of Eden, there was always an opportunity for rebellion.

153
00:13:56,320 --> 00:14:00,960
There was always the tree of the knowledge of good and evil for us to take and eat.

154
00:14:00,960 --> 00:14:03,420
If Adam and Eve didn't do it, somebody would have done it.

155
00:14:05,900 --> 00:14:13,320
Because in the new heaven and the new earth, it's filled with people who have bowed their knees in submission and reverence to King Jesus.

156
00:14:13,320 --> 00:14:17,280
There will be no rebellion in the new heaven and the new earth.

157
00:14:17,280 --> 00:14:23,220
The new heaven and the new earth is the fullness of God's recreation, goodness restored eternally.

158
00:14:23,220 --> 00:14:27,900
But it's still a process.

159
00:14:27,900 --> 00:14:30,240
We're still in the middle of it.

160
00:14:30,240 --> 00:14:38,420
But we're in between God's original good design, the brokenness of it, us participating regularly in redefining his goodness.

161
00:14:38,420 --> 00:14:41,820
And eventually he's going to restore it.

162
00:14:41,820 --> 00:14:49,820
But for now, we're in this process where God's partially and slowly working his plan for recreation.

163
00:14:49,820 --> 00:14:53,660
We see this start in the Old Testament, obviously through the nation of Israel.

164
00:14:53,660 --> 00:14:58,860
And God is working his plan for recreation through the nation of Israel.

165
00:14:58,860 --> 00:15:01,360
So it's going to lead us all the way up to Jesus' birth.

166
00:15:01,360 --> 00:15:04,400
If you've been in church a long time, you know all of this.

167
00:15:04,400 --> 00:15:11,780
But we see one key character in this recreation process that I want to focus on this morning.

168
00:15:11,780 --> 00:15:12,560
His name is Moses.

169
00:15:12,560 --> 00:15:14,380
You guys familiar with Moses, right?

170
00:15:14,380 --> 00:15:19,580
So Moses is leading the people of Israel out of slavery in Egypt.

171
00:15:19,580 --> 00:15:21,920
And God parts the Red Sea.

172
00:15:21,920 --> 00:15:23,860
They walk across on dry land.

173
00:15:23,980 --> 00:15:26,000
All is going well.

174
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:39,800
And Moses goes up on Mount Sinai to meet with God and to hear from God and to get some good laws from God that are going to help the nation of Israel live into God's goodness here in this broken world.

175
00:15:39,920 --> 00:15:44,160
And before Moses even comes down the mountain, what does Israel do?

176
00:15:44,160 --> 00:15:48,360
They start redefining God's goodness in their own image.

177
00:15:48,360 --> 00:15:53,980
They create a golden path that they can control, a religious system that they can be in charge of.

178
00:15:53,980 --> 00:15:57,200
They're redefining God's goodness in his image.

179
00:15:57,420 --> 00:16:00,760
And finally, God's like, all right, Moses, I've had it with these people.

180
00:16:00,760 --> 00:16:03,000
I'm going to have to take a break.

181
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:06,440
Maybe you and I can recreate a new nation or something.

182
00:16:06,440 --> 00:16:09,020
And Moses is like, no, God, you made this promise to Abraham.

183
00:16:09,020 --> 00:16:11,020
You've been faithful to your people.

184
00:16:11,020 --> 00:16:13,300
You've got to keep being faithful.

185
00:16:13,300 --> 00:16:16,160
It's a really interesting conversation.

186
00:16:16,160 --> 00:16:21,320
If you haven't read it, Exodus chapter 33 is one of the most quoted chapters in all of the Bible.

187
00:16:21,320 --> 00:16:24,000
The Bible, New Testament writers quote it a lot.

188
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:25,780
Other Old Testament writers quote it a lot.

189
00:16:25,780 --> 00:16:27,460
It's a fun conversation.

190
00:16:27,460 --> 00:16:37,240
But at the climax of it, Moses decides that he wants to see God's presence, his glory, face to face.

191
00:16:37,240 --> 00:16:40,340
He wants to know who God is.

192
00:16:40,340 --> 00:16:42,160
And so he's having this conversation with God.

193
00:16:42,160 --> 00:16:45,180
And this is what he says in Exodus chapter 33, verse 18.

194
00:16:45,180 --> 00:16:49,980
Moses responded to God, show me your glorious presence.

195
00:16:49,980 --> 00:16:55,100
Essentially what Moses is saying here is I want to see you face to face.

196
00:16:55,100 --> 00:16:56,400
I want to know what you're like.

197
00:16:56,400 --> 00:16:57,660
I want to know you intimately.

198
00:16:57,660 --> 00:17:02,220
I want to understand fully who you are and what you're like.

199
00:17:02,220 --> 00:17:03,760
Show me your glory.

200
00:17:03,760 --> 00:17:06,080
And what is God's response?

201
00:17:06,080 --> 00:17:10,460
I will make my goodness pass before you.

202
00:17:10,460 --> 00:17:13,780
And I will call out to you my name, Yahweh.

203
00:17:13,780 --> 00:17:17,060
For I will show mercy to anyone I choose.

204
00:17:17,060 --> 00:17:19,720
And I will show compassion to anyone I choose.

205
00:17:20,140 --> 00:17:23,820
But you may not look at me directly or see my face.

206
00:17:23,820 --> 00:17:33,940
So Moses wants to see all of God's fullness, who he is, what he's like, God's glory.

207
00:17:33,940 --> 00:17:38,580
And God says all of my fullness can be summarized by my goodness.

208
00:17:38,580 --> 00:17:44,620
And I'm going to show you some of it partially, but I can't show it to you all because if you see it, your face will melt off.

209
00:17:45,560 --> 00:17:54,820
Like this idea of goodness, God's goodness is so vast, so big, so overwhelming that human minds can't fully comprehend it.

210
00:17:54,820 --> 00:17:59,380
We've turned it into this cliche word that we use and we put on coffee mugs.

211
00:17:59,380 --> 00:18:05,140
But at the end of the day, God's goodness is the summarization of who he is.

212
00:18:06,320 --> 00:18:12,620
He tells Moses, you want to see who I am, you want to see what I'm like, well, let me show you my goodness.

213
00:18:12,620 --> 00:18:19,760
And this passage is where I want to derive my definition for goodness this morning.

214
00:18:19,760 --> 00:18:32,900
I would say that God's goodness or this goodness that we're talking about in Galatians chapter 5 is the summarization of all of God's attributes and actions bound together in a single word.

215
00:18:34,200 --> 00:18:39,760
It's radical, overwhelming, and even impossible for us to wrap our minds around.

216
00:18:39,760 --> 00:18:47,320
And if we're going to be a people that the Holy Spirit produces goodness in our life, it can't come from us.

217
00:18:47,320 --> 00:18:51,700
It has to come from a radical dependence on the Holy Spirit.

218
00:18:51,700 --> 00:18:55,280
This is not one of those things that we can fake.

219
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:57,280
We can fake patience.

220
00:18:57,280 --> 00:18:59,940
We can fake kindness sometimes, right?

221
00:18:59,940 --> 00:19:01,340
But we can't fake goodness.

222
00:19:01,840 --> 00:19:08,840
Goodness is the whole expression of all of God's attributes and actions bound together in a single word.

223
00:19:08,840 --> 00:19:13,540
We have to be totally reliant on the Holy Spirit for this to happen.

224
00:19:15,680 --> 00:19:25,740
I'm going to do my best to illustrate this, knowing that any time you try and illustrate a characteristic of God, that it's possible that the illustration is going to fall short, right?

225
00:19:25,740 --> 00:19:30,680
Because he tells Moses, you can't really comprehend it if you do your face will melt off.

226
00:19:30,680 --> 00:19:34,660
So my silly illustration is probably going to fall short, but I hope that it helps.

227
00:19:34,660 --> 00:19:38,560
You got, when you got walked in, you probably got one of these clips, right?

228
00:19:38,560 --> 00:19:39,320
Everybody get a clip?

229
00:19:40,060 --> 00:19:41,400
Hold up your clip if you have it.

230
00:19:41,400 --> 00:19:41,680
Great.

231
00:19:41,680 --> 00:19:49,120
So what I have here is I got a couple stacks of cardstock listed on it.

232
00:19:49,120 --> 00:19:52,360
Attributes of God and actions of God.

233
00:19:52,360 --> 00:19:54,280
We've got God's grace.

234
00:19:54,280 --> 00:19:55,800
Everybody likes God's grace, right?

235
00:19:55,800 --> 00:19:56,700
He's one of our favorite.

236
00:19:56,700 --> 00:19:57,360
God's grace.

237
00:19:57,360 --> 00:19:58,880
He's a gracious God.

238
00:19:58,880 --> 00:20:00,700
God's faithful.

239
00:20:00,700 --> 00:20:03,140
Everybody loves God's faithfulness, right?

240
00:20:03,180 --> 00:20:04,120
He's faithful to us.

241
00:20:04,120 --> 00:20:04,760
He's consistent.

242
00:20:04,760 --> 00:20:05,900
He's steadfast.

243
00:20:05,900 --> 00:20:06,900
We're like that.

244
00:20:06,900 --> 00:20:08,320
God's patient.

245
00:20:08,320 --> 00:20:11,000
Long-suffering.

246
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:12,040
He's powerful.

247
00:20:12,040 --> 00:20:14,060
He splits the Red Sea.

248
00:20:14,060 --> 00:20:16,680
He makes the sun stand still.

249
00:20:16,680 --> 00:20:18,180
He's just.

250
00:20:18,180 --> 00:20:23,820
He cares about what's right and good and making sure that that happens.

251
00:20:23,820 --> 00:20:24,960
He's wise.

252
00:20:24,960 --> 00:20:27,200
He's sovereign.

253
00:20:27,200 --> 00:20:29,240
He's in control of all things.

254
00:20:29,240 --> 00:20:30,360
He's holy.

255
00:20:30,360 --> 00:20:32,060
He's righteous.

256
00:20:32,920 --> 00:20:35,220
We have a lot of attributes from God, right?

257
00:20:35,220 --> 00:20:36,480
And then we've got actions from God.

258
00:20:36,480 --> 00:20:40,020
We see him interact with his people in the Old Testament, in the New Testament.

259
00:20:40,020 --> 00:20:44,760
We see him interact with guys like King David, and David's sinful and messes up all the time,

260
00:20:44,760 --> 00:20:47,200
and God's just faithful and patient and gracious with him.

261
00:20:47,200 --> 00:20:52,240
We see King Solomon, who's got all of these wives, and we're like, why is he, why does God work with this guy?

262
00:20:52,240 --> 00:20:53,500
He's doing all this crazy stuff.

263
00:20:53,500 --> 00:20:54,080
That's crazy.

264
00:20:54,080 --> 00:20:55,880
And then we've got Babylon, right?

265
00:20:55,880 --> 00:21:02,520
We've got God allowing his people to go into slavery from a wicked and cruel nation.

266
00:21:02,660 --> 00:21:04,700
We see power in the Red Sea.

267
00:21:04,700 --> 00:21:07,180
We see all of these incredible actions.

268
00:21:07,180 --> 00:21:10,040
We see him bringing Israel out of slavery in Egypt.

269
00:21:10,040 --> 00:21:12,740
We see the walls of Jericho falling down.

270
00:21:12,740 --> 00:21:16,220
So we have a list of all of these actions and attributes of God.

271
00:21:16,220 --> 00:21:33,600
And what I believe goodness is, is I believe goodness is all of these things bound together in a single word.

272
00:21:33,600 --> 00:21:34,600
But what do we do?

273
00:21:34,600 --> 00:21:42,160
What do we do as Christians when we find ourselves in situations where we know God is calling us to goodness?

274
00:21:42,160 --> 00:21:46,140
He's calling us to manifest goodness in our lives.

275
00:21:46,740 --> 00:21:54,740
But what we tend to do is, depending on the situation, we tend to pick and choose the attributes and actions that fit our narrative and agenda.

276
00:21:56,620 --> 00:22:01,520
So when you're about to sin, you're tempted to sin, and you know it's coming, and you're like, I know I shouldn't do this.

277
00:22:01,520 --> 00:22:03,000
I shouldn't gossip about my coworker.

278
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,260
I shouldn't have this extra beer.

279
00:22:05,260 --> 00:22:06,400
I shouldn't do this thing.

280
00:22:06,400 --> 00:22:08,920
I shouldn't say this about this person or whatever it is.

281
00:22:08,920 --> 00:22:10,140
You've got that temptation to sin.

282
00:22:10,140 --> 00:22:11,220
You know you're about to do it.

283
00:22:12,240 --> 00:22:14,900
And you think to yourself, well, hmm, let me see here.

284
00:22:14,900 --> 00:22:16,200
We've got God's grace.

285
00:22:16,200 --> 00:22:17,760
God's a gracious God, right?

286
00:22:17,760 --> 00:22:19,180
And we've got King David.

287
00:22:19,180 --> 00:22:23,380
Man, King David was a wicked dude, and God just kept forgiving him over and over and over again.

288
00:22:23,380 --> 00:22:29,780
So what I'm going to do, and I'm just going to sin anyway because God's gracious and because God's good to sinners like King David.

289
00:22:29,780 --> 00:22:31,460
So I'm just going to do that.

290
00:22:31,460 --> 00:22:36,420
Or someone sins against us.

291
00:22:36,420 --> 00:22:38,620
And then we're like, screw this grace thing.

292
00:22:38,620 --> 00:22:39,300
Where's the justice?

293
00:22:39,300 --> 00:22:40,180
Where's the justice?

294
00:22:40,180 --> 00:22:41,520
The justice is here somewhere.

295
00:22:42,240 --> 00:22:43,720
I know it's here somewhere.

296
00:22:43,720 --> 00:22:45,220
We've got God's justice.

297
00:22:45,220 --> 00:22:46,640
Here we go.

298
00:22:46,640 --> 00:22:47,440
God's just.

299
00:22:47,440 --> 00:22:49,740
And then we've got, like, the walls of Jericho falling down.

300
00:22:49,740 --> 00:22:53,640
We're like, God brings down mountains of stone on his enemies.

301
00:22:53,640 --> 00:22:57,400
And so my enemies are going to get God's justice and his power.

302
00:22:57,400 --> 00:22:58,900
And so we pick and choose.

303
00:22:58,900 --> 00:22:59,780
Do you see what we do here?

304
00:22:59,780 --> 00:23:01,760
Am I the only one that does this?

305
00:23:01,760 --> 00:23:07,000
Am I the only one that picks and chooses God's attributes and actions that fit my agenda?

306
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:12,060
See, Romans chapter 1 is actually about non-Christians.

307
00:23:12,240 --> 00:23:14,180
We're redefining God's goodness in our own image.

308
00:23:14,180 --> 00:23:20,040
But Christians, we actually kind of use God, parts of God.

309
00:23:20,040 --> 00:23:26,160
And we actually, thinking that we're righteous or more holy than other people, we do the same thing that they do with just a twist.

310
00:23:26,480 --> 00:23:33,060
We take parts of God's actions and attributes, the ones that fit our agenda and narrative, and we do those things.

311
00:23:34,280 --> 00:23:40,740
Maybe your friend's caught up in a pattern of sin, and you know you should say something to them.

312
00:23:40,740 --> 00:23:43,140
Like, you know you have that type of relationship.

313
00:23:43,140 --> 00:23:43,740
You love them.

314
00:23:43,740 --> 00:23:44,300
You care about them.

315
00:23:44,300 --> 00:23:46,440
You see them going down a path they shouldn't go down.

316
00:23:47,960 --> 00:23:51,580
And you feel the Holy Spirit prompting you to have a conversation.

317
00:23:51,580 --> 00:23:57,000
But instead, because you don't want to have the conversation, you know it's going to be hard.

318
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:58,660
You know it's going to be difficult.

319
00:23:58,660 --> 00:24:02,060
You know it might not go the way that you want it to go.

320
00:24:02,060 --> 00:24:03,760
You remind yourself of God's patience.

321
00:24:03,760 --> 00:24:07,500
You remind yourself of Solomon and his 700 wives.

322
00:24:07,640 --> 00:24:14,580
And you're like, God didn't say anything to Solomon, so maybe I shouldn't say anything to this person that I care about, that I love.

323
00:24:14,580 --> 00:24:22,980
Even though we know the Holy Spirit is prompting us, the good thing to do is to find where God's actions and attributes are bound together

324
00:24:22,980 --> 00:24:27,680
and to be led by the Holy Spirit to do the good thing that God wants us to do in the moment.

325
00:24:27,680 --> 00:24:33,640
But instead, we redefine goodness in our image to fit our agendas and our narratives

326
00:24:33,640 --> 00:24:37,220
so that we don't actually have to do the thing that we know God's calling us to do.

327
00:24:37,640 --> 00:24:48,820
Goodness, it's all of God's actions and all of his attributes bound together in a single word.

328
00:24:48,820 --> 00:24:59,560
But if we're honest, sometimes it's hard to take how God interacted with the nation of Israel

329
00:24:59,560 --> 00:25:03,000
and apply that directly to our lives, right?

330
00:25:03,360 --> 00:25:10,340
Like Yahweh interacting with the Old Testament doesn't always necessarily fit our lives.

331
00:25:10,340 --> 00:25:16,360
And so God does this incredibly good thing in his plan for recreation, in his process of recreation,

332
00:25:16,360 --> 00:25:22,720
not just working with the nation of Israel, but then he sends his one and only son in the flesh

333
00:25:22,720 --> 00:25:26,540
to manifest what it looks like for God to live in a human body,

334
00:25:27,360 --> 00:25:30,820
for us to see what goodness looks like in a person.

335
00:25:30,820 --> 00:25:37,780
God sends Jesus, his goodness perfectly demonstrated in the person of Jesus.

336
00:25:37,780 --> 00:25:43,360
Colossians 1, verse 15 says that Christ is the visible image of the invisible God.

337
00:25:43,360 --> 00:25:45,440
Do we want to know what goodness looks like?

338
00:25:45,440 --> 00:25:50,880
Do we want to know what all of God's actions and attributes bound together in a single person looks like?

339
00:25:50,920 --> 00:25:52,220
It looks like Jesus.

340
00:25:52,220 --> 00:25:55,840
John 14, 9, Jesus says,

341
00:25:55,840 --> 00:25:59,440
Anyone who has seen me has seen the Father.

342
00:25:59,440 --> 00:26:03,280
Do we want to know what goodness looks like?

343
00:26:03,280 --> 00:26:07,040
Do we want to know what all of God's actions and attributes look like?

344
00:26:07,040 --> 00:26:08,840
Look like Jesus.

345
00:26:08,840 --> 00:26:16,300
So what does Jesus say about goodness and how does he demonstrate it in his life?

346
00:26:16,460 --> 00:26:20,420
Well, he says one thing that's pretty profound about goodness.

347
00:26:20,420 --> 00:26:25,680
This rich young ruler comes to him and wants to know how to inherit eternal life.

348
00:26:25,680 --> 00:26:26,160
He says,

349
00:26:26,160 --> 00:26:31,340
Good teacher, what must I do to have eternal life?

350
00:26:31,340 --> 00:26:32,380
And Jesus says to him,

351
00:26:32,380 --> 00:26:33,700
Why do you call me good?

352
00:26:33,700 --> 00:26:38,460
For no one can be good except for God.

353
00:26:39,700 --> 00:26:47,160
So again, I hope you're seeing that this task that Paul is asking us to do,

354
00:26:47,160 --> 00:26:50,700
he's asking us to manifest goodness in our life that it's impossible,

355
00:26:50,700 --> 00:26:56,720
that Jesus himself says no one can be good except for God.

356
00:26:56,720 --> 00:26:59,960
Because how are we who don't have the mind of God,

357
00:26:59,960 --> 00:27:02,180
who aren't sovereign and in control,

358
00:27:02,180 --> 00:27:03,440
we can't read people's hearts.

359
00:27:03,440 --> 00:27:05,760
So often we see Jesus interact with people.

360
00:27:06,080 --> 00:27:10,080
Part of his interaction is him knowing what's in their hearts or knowing what's in their minds.

361
00:27:10,080 --> 00:27:10,940
We don't have that.

362
00:27:10,940 --> 00:27:15,140
So it's an impossible task that we're being asked to do,

363
00:27:15,140 --> 00:27:19,460
which is why I think we have to be radically dependent on the Holy Spirit.

364
00:27:19,460 --> 00:27:26,920
So often we find ourselves in situations and we want to know what God would do.

365
00:27:26,920 --> 00:27:29,300
We want to know what God would have us to do.

366
00:27:29,300 --> 00:27:33,560
And instead of praying and being dependent on the Holy Spirit,

367
00:27:33,560 --> 00:27:37,540
we just go back to our head knowledge and we try and find the proof text

368
00:27:37,540 --> 00:27:41,460
that can allow us to do the thing that we want to do.

369
00:27:41,460 --> 00:27:45,800
We pick and choose his actions and his attributes that fit our agenda and our narrative.

370
00:27:45,800 --> 00:27:51,420
And I just want to say I believe that the goodness of God is so much bigger than that.

371
00:27:51,420 --> 00:27:56,680
I believe that the goodness of God is going to take some serious prayer,

372
00:27:56,680 --> 00:27:58,620
some serious reliance on the Holy Spirit.

373
00:27:58,620 --> 00:28:01,800
Jesus says that he's going to send his spirit and that through his spirit,

374
00:28:01,800 --> 00:28:05,060
his followers will do far greater things than he did.

375
00:28:05,060 --> 00:28:07,600
Do we take that seriously?

376
00:28:07,600 --> 00:28:13,720
Do we seriously think that the Holy Spirit can produce God's goodness in our lives?

377
00:28:13,720 --> 00:28:19,000
Do we seriously think that we can bind all of these things together

378
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:22,960
in nuance, in relationship with our neighbors and our coworkers and our friends?

379
00:28:24,400 --> 00:28:30,100
The reality is we can't do it unless we're trusting in the Holy Spirit to give us wisdom

380
00:28:30,100 --> 00:28:33,020
for each situation and circumstance we find ourselves in.

381
00:28:33,020 --> 00:28:35,900
If we're not dependent on the Holy Spirit, if we're not praying,

382
00:28:35,900 --> 00:28:39,740
if we're not asking God for wisdom, what we'll do is we'll pick and choose the stories

383
00:28:39,740 --> 00:28:42,820
that allow us to do the thing that we want to do.

384
00:28:43,680 --> 00:28:49,360
And I don't know about you, but far too often, far too often,

385
00:28:49,360 --> 00:28:53,600
I'm really good at finding the things that justify the actions that I want to do.

386
00:28:55,760 --> 00:29:00,780
And so God, he sends us Jesus to be the perfect manifestation of goodness,

387
00:29:00,780 --> 00:29:05,220
to be all of God's actions and attributes bound up in a single word.

388
00:29:05,220 --> 00:29:10,140
We see Jesus having interactions with a woman caught in adultery.

389
00:29:10,140 --> 00:29:14,400
We see him with a soldier in the garden.

390
00:29:14,400 --> 00:29:15,860
Peter chops his ear off.

391
00:29:15,860 --> 00:29:18,280
This guy's about to take Jesus to the cross,

392
00:29:18,280 --> 00:29:22,060
and Jesus lovingly picks up his ear and puts it back on.

393
00:29:24,160 --> 00:29:26,880
We see Jesus with a religious leader, Nicodemus,

394
00:29:26,880 --> 00:29:29,480
and he seems to be pretty gracious with Nicodemus.

395
00:29:29,480 --> 00:29:35,400
He says, for God so loved you that he sent his one and only son,

396
00:29:35,400 --> 00:29:39,800
that whoever might believe in him would have eternal life.

397
00:29:39,800 --> 00:29:42,080
He seems to have a lot of grace for Nicodemus,

398
00:29:42,080 --> 00:29:45,420
but then later he calls some other religious leaders whitewashed tombs.

399
00:29:45,420 --> 00:29:47,720
How do we reconcile those two things?

400
00:29:47,720 --> 00:29:51,600
How do we reconcile God at night sitting with Nicodemus being gracious,

401
00:29:51,600 --> 00:29:54,020
reminding him of God's goodness and his faithfulness,

402
00:29:54,080 --> 00:29:59,660
and then Jesus calling the religious leaders whitewashed tombs or flipping tables in the temple?

403
00:29:59,660 --> 00:30:03,860
How do we reconcile all of these things?

404
00:30:03,860 --> 00:30:11,680
It's all of God's actions and attributes bound together in a single word,

405
00:30:11,680 --> 00:30:13,820
and the only way that we're going to manifest this,

406
00:30:13,820 --> 00:30:18,180
the only way that we're going to do this well in relationship with our neighbors,

407
00:30:18,320 --> 00:30:22,860
our co-workers and our friends is if the Holy Spirit is working in us and through us.

408
00:30:22,860 --> 00:30:28,500
If we are getting to know Jesus, like actually spending time with Jesus,

409
00:30:28,500 --> 00:30:34,960
and if we're living in community with other brothers and sisters who also know Jesus,

410
00:30:34,960 --> 00:30:37,580
but look at this, think differently than we do,

411
00:30:38,640 --> 00:30:43,880
because Jesus is a pretty profound, all-consuming religious leader,

412
00:30:43,880 --> 00:30:45,840
and we tend to have blind spots, right?

413
00:30:45,840 --> 00:30:49,680
Like we tend to see things that we want to see and ignore things that we don't want to see,

414
00:30:49,680 --> 00:30:54,220
and so it would be good if we, as the church gathered together with other believers

415
00:30:54,220 --> 00:30:56,960
who think differently about us but who also know Jesus,

416
00:30:57,500 --> 00:31:04,260
and then we radically rely on the Holy Spirit to lead us into his goodness.

417
00:31:04,260 --> 00:31:11,900
So what does this look like?

418
00:31:11,900 --> 00:31:18,140
We've got to stop picking and choosing the actions and attributes of God that best fit our narratives.

419
00:31:18,140 --> 00:31:22,840
We've got to stop redefining God's goodness in our own image.

420
00:31:22,840 --> 00:31:26,440
We've got to radically rely on the Holy Spirit.

421
00:31:26,500 --> 00:31:28,600
This is what I was talking about at the beginning of the message,

422
00:31:28,600 --> 00:31:33,920
that I feel like God's goodness is opening up a door that we're going to spend the rest of our lives trying to figure out,

423
00:31:33,920 --> 00:31:40,360
the rest of our lives trying to rely on the Holy Spirit to produce this fruit in our lives.

424
00:31:40,360 --> 00:31:44,040
It's not something that we can just add a couple practical steps into our life,

425
00:31:44,040 --> 00:31:45,520
and we'll be better at being good.

426
00:31:45,520 --> 00:31:47,800
It doesn't work that way.

427
00:31:47,800 --> 00:31:51,160
Our tendency is to redefine God's goodness in our own image,

428
00:31:51,160 --> 00:31:53,340
and so if we're going to produce God's goodness in our life,

429
00:31:53,340 --> 00:31:56,000
we have to be radically dependent on his Holy Spirit.

430
00:31:56,500 --> 00:32:03,160
So hopefully you take these clips with you and you put them somewhere,

431
00:32:03,160 --> 00:32:09,020
and you remember the holistic nature of God's goodness.

432
00:32:09,020 --> 00:32:14,240
You remember that it's all of his actions and attributes bound up in a single word.

433
00:32:14,360 --> 00:32:20,600
You remember that in order for you to be good, it means being perfect.

434
00:32:20,600 --> 00:32:27,640
It means perfectly balancing all of God's actions and attributes in every situation and scenario you find yourself in,

435
00:32:27,640 --> 00:32:29,040
and you cannot do that.

436
00:32:31,540 --> 00:32:37,160
I define God's goodness as all of his actions and attributes summarized in a word.

437
00:32:37,160 --> 00:32:38,580
What does that look like lived out?

438
00:32:38,580 --> 00:32:40,720
How do we live that?

